{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss Arial;}{\f1\fswiss Calibri;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\f1\fs36\b NETSCOPE\par\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\qc\f1\fs22 Complete Project Documentation\par\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ql\f1\fs20\par\b\fs26 1. Project Overview\par\pard\fs20 NETSCOPE is a Flask-based IT help desk and asset management system designed to support administrators, staff, and technicians in one integrated platform. The project is intended for internal IT support operations and asset tracking within a small-to-medium organization. It allows staff members to report issues, administrators to assign technicians and manage inventory, and technicians to resolve work requests while recording diagnostic and maintenance details.\par\par The system combines four core functions: ticket management, asset tracking, role-based access control, and maintenance record keeping. It is built using Flask, SQLAlchemy, Flask-Login, Flask-SocketIO, and Jinja2 templates, with SQLite used as the local database engine.\par\par\b\fs26 2. Problem Statement\par\pard\fs20 Organizations often rely on informal communication methods such as email, phone calls, and manual spreadsheets to manage IT requests. These methods are hard to monitor, difficult to audit, and often lead to missed updates and inconsistent asset tracking. Without a centralized system, administrators may not know which tickets are open, which assets are assigned, or which technicians are active.\par\par NETSCOPE addresses these issues by providing a single digital environment for issue reporting, ticket resolution, asset assignment, and user role management.\par\par\b\fs26 3. Aim and Objectives\par\pard\fs20\b Aim:\b0  To develop a functional IT help desk and asset management system using Flask and SQLite.\par\par\b Objectives:\b0\par - Design a multi-role application with admin, staff, and technician access.\par - Implement user authentication and authorization.\par - Provide asset management workflow.\par - Allow staff to create and monitor tickets.\par - Support technician assignment and ticket resolution.\par - Record private ticket chat and maintenance activity.\par - Provide dashboards for monitoring system performance.\par\par\b\fs26 4. Scope\par\pard\fs20 The project covers:\par - User registration and authentication\par - Role-based authorization\par - Asset creation, assignment, editing, and tracking\par - Ticket creation and management\par - Ticket assignment to technicians\par - Ticket status updates and resolution notes\par - Private ticket chat between staff and assigned technician\par - Admin notifications for new tickets\par - Maintenance logging for asset-related work\par - Dashboard summaries and charts\par\par The application is designed for local or internal deployment rather than enterprise production hosting.\par\par\b\fs26 5. System Features\par\pard\fs20 The current implementation includes:\par - Flask application factory architecture\par - SQLAlchemy models for users, tickets, assets, notifications, chat messages, and maintenance logs\par - Role-based route protection\par - Ticket submission and assignment workflow\par - Live notifications for new tickets\par - Private ticket communication using Socket.IO\par - Asset inventory management and assignment\par - Technician resolution forms and status updates\par - SQLite persistence for local deployment\par\par\b\fs26 6. Roles and Permissions\par\pard\fs20 The application defines three user roles:\par\par - Admin: manages users, assets, tickets, assignment, and dashboard metrics.\par - Staff: creates tickets and views assigned assets.\par - Technician: reviews assigned tickets, updates work status, and records resolution details.\par\par Public registration is intentionally restricted to staff and technician users. Admin accounts are created through an administrative command or super-admin workflow rather than public registration.\par\par\b\fs26 7. Architecture\par\pard\fs20 NETSCOPE follows the Flask application factory design. The application is divided into modular components, including:\par\par - application factory in the main package\par - database model definitions\par - route blueprints for auth, admin, staff, and technician\par - Jinja2 templates for role-specific interfaces\par - static assets including CSS, JavaScript, and images\par - event handlers for notifications and chat\par\par The project structure reflects separation of concerns, allowing user interaction, business logic, templates, and database operations to remain organized and manageable.\par\par\b\fs26 8. Application Workflow\par\pard\fs20 A standard user flow works as follows:\par\par 1. A user logs in from the authentication page.\par 2. The system redirects the user to the correct dashboard based on role.\par 3. A staff member creates a ticket with issue details and support requirements.\par 4. Administrators receive notifications and assign a technician.\par 5. The technician starts work and updates ticket status.\par 6. The technician records resolution details, actions taken, and outcome.\par 7. Staff and technician communicate through a private ticket conversation.\par 8. The administrator monitors system metrics and asset status through the dashboard.\par\par\b\fs26 9. Database Design\par\pard\fs20 The application uses SQLite with Flask-SQLAlchemy. The main relational models are:\par\par - User\par - Asset\par - Ticket\par - TicketMessage\par - Notification\par - MaintenanceLog\par\par\b User\b0:\par Stores login credentials, role, account status, and metadata such as creation date. Each user may create tickets, receive notifications, or be assigned as technician or staff member.\par\par\b Asset\b0:\par Represents inventory-related devices and equipment. Fields include asset tag, name, category, brand, model, serial number, department, status, location, assignment, and purchase date.\par\par\b Ticket\b0:\par Stores the issue being reported. Fields include title, description, priority, category, support type, remote support identifier, assigned technician, status, and resolution details.\par\par\b TicketMessage\b0:\par Stores private ticket chat entries exchanged between the ticket creator and the assigned technician.\par\par\b Notification\b0:\par Stores alert messages sent to administrators when new tickets are created or work changes occur.\par\par\b MaintenanceLog\b0:\par Records maintenance and service actions performed on assets by technicians.\par\par The database relationship model supports many-to-one and one-to-many patterns between users, assets, tickets, notifications, and maintenance records.\par\par\b\fs26 10. Key Project Data Fields\par\pard\fs20 The implementation includes the following important domain fields:\par\par - Ticket support type: On-site or Remote\par - Remote support ID: AnyDesk or remote access identifier\par - Resolution outcome: Resolved or Unable to Resolve\par - Asset serial number and asset tag\par - Department and assigned staff relationship\par - Ticket status values including Open, In Progress, Resolved, and Closed\par - Notification status and unread tracking\par\par\b\fs26 11. Admin Workflow\par\pard\fs20 Administrators use the dashboard to monitor overall performance and manage the system. Typical tasks include:\par\par - Reviewing ticket queues\par - Assigning tickets to technicians\par - Managing user accounts\par - Adding, editing, and deleting assets\par - Assigning assets to staff\par - Reviewing ticket details and updates\par - Tracking maintenance and resolution records\par\par Admin users can also receive new ticket notifications and open the relevant support item directly from those notifications.\par\par\b\fs26 12. Staff Workflow\par\pard\fs20 Staff users sign in and can:\par\par - view assigned assets\par - create IT support tickets\par - view tickets they created\par - inspect ticket status and technician assignment\par - participate in the private ticket chat\par\par When creating a ticket, a staff member provides title, description, category, priority, support type, and remote ID if the issue is remote-based.\par\par\b\fs26 13. Technician Workflow\par\pard\fs20 Technicians work with the ticket queue assigned to them. Their tasks include:\par\par - viewing assigned tickets\par - starting work on open tickets\par - changing status to In Progress\par - documenting diagnostic steps\par - recording actions taken\par - writing resolution notes\par - marking the outcome as resolved or unable to resolve\par - logging maintenance activities on assets\par\par Technicians also maintain a private ticket communication thread with the staff requester.\par\par\b\fs26 14. Route Overview\par\pard\fs20 The application includes role-based routes for public, admin, staff, and technician access. The main route groups include:\par\par - Login and registration routes\par - Admin dashboard and management routes\par - Staff ticket and asset routes\par - Technician queue and resolution routes\par - Ticket messaging endpoints\par - App-level dashboard redirect route\par\par These routes are protected by login and role checks to ensure only valid users can access the appropriate features.\par\par\b\fs26 15. Security Model\par\pard\fs20 NETSCOPE uses Flask-Login to manage sessions and secure route access. Passwords are stored as hashed values rather than plain text. Role checks ensure administrators do not have access to unauthorized staff-only routes, and staff can be restricted from technician-only features.\par\par A local development configuration disables CSRF protection to avoid false mismatch errors in the lab environment. This is acceptable for local testing but should be reviewed before production deployment.\par\par\b\fs26 16. Testing and Validation\par\pard\fs20 A pytest suite is included in the project and covers authentication, permissions, asset management, ticket creation, ticket assignment, status changes, and maintenance logging.\par\par The executed test result in the current environment was:\par - 16 tests passed\par - exit code 0\par\par This confirms the core implemented workflows are functioning correctly for the current local setup.\par\par\b\fs26 17. Local Setup and Usage\par\pard\fs20 The project can be run locally by creating a virtual environment, installing dependencies, configuring environment variables, initializing the database, creating an admin account, and starting the app.\par\par Typical setup steps include:\par - creating a Python virtual environment\par - installing requirements\par - setting the secret key and database URL\par - running the database initialization command\par - creating the admin account\par - starting the Flask application\par\par The project is intended to be used at a local address such as:\par http://127.0.0.1:5000\par\par\b\fs26 18. Deployment Guidance\par\pard\fs20 The application is optimized for local and academic deployment. It uses SQLite and environment variables, which makes it easy to run on a developer machine or hosted Python environment.\par\par For production-grade deployment, the system should be reviewed for:\par - secure secret key management\par - stronger database selection\par - HTTPS enforcement\par - hardened CSRF and session controls\par - proper migration strategy\par - monitoring and logging\par\par The project is best described as a functional internal support system rather than a fully hardened enterprise production stack.\par\par\b\fs26 19. Known Notes and Limitations\par\pard\fs20 The current codebase includes some practical project notes:\par\par - local development environment is used for verification\par - SQLite is used instead of a production database\par - public registration is restricted for admin accounts\par - stale server cleanup may be necessary when restarting locally\par - CSRF is disabled in the local dev configuration\par\par These considerations are part of the implementation as it currently exists and do not invalidate the functionality of the application.\par\par\b\fs26 20. Summary\par\pard\fs20 NETSCOPE successfully implements a role-based IT help desk and asset management system suited to academic demonstration and local operational use. It integrates support ticket creation, technician assignment, asset tracking, private communication, notifications, permission control, and reporting into a single web application.\par\par The project is complete in its current form, documented in its documentation set, and verified through a passing pytest suite.\par\par\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\qc\f1\fs16 This document was consolidated from the project documentation set for Word-compatible presentation.\par}